          deepDive: "**Nのごとく** is the literary counterpart of **Nのように**: *like N, as N* (the book: 〜のように). It comes from the classical auxiliary ごとし (*be like*), and all three forms survive in written Japanese (the ＊ note):\n- **Nのごとく** + verb (= のように): 薄紙のごとく透け始めた (*began to turn transparent like thin paper* — the sample), 影のごとく寄り添う (*stick close like a shadow*), 湯水のごとく使う (*spend like water*).\n- **N₁のごとき N₂** (= のような): 鉄のごとき信念 (*an iron will*), 炎のごとき一生 (*a life like a blazing flame* — the Check).\n- **Nのごとし** (= のようだ), mostly in proverbs: 光陰矢のごとし (*time flies like an arrow* — hence the idiom mark).\n\nTwo uses (どう使う？):\n- **Simile**: 風のごとく走り去った (*ran off like the wind*), 彗星のごとく現れた (*appeared out of nowhere like a comet*), 猫のごとく (*stealthily, like a cat*).\n- **Giving the content / an example**: 次のごとく, 下記のごとく, 以下のごとく, 前述のごとく (*as follows / as stated below / as mentioned above*): 遺書には次のごとく記されていた (*the will read as follows*). Common in formal documents and minutes.\n\nThe 📎 note: after a **clause** (plain form), ごとく attaches directly or with が / かの:\n- **Vる + （が）ごとく**: 草を薙ぎ払うがごとく (*as if mowing down grass*), 舞うごとく (*as if dancing*), 眠るがごとく (*as if falling asleep*); がごとく is more classical.\n- **Plain form + かのごとく** = かのように, *as if (though it isn't so)*: 明日死ぬかのごとく生きよ (*live as if you were to die tomorrow*), 何事もなかったかのごとく (*as if nothing had happened*). With なA / N, だ becomes である: 社長であるかのごとく威張る (*swagger as if he were the president*); ✗社長だかのごとく.\n\nCompare:\n- **〜のように / ような / ようだ**: everyday. ごとく is written, formal or deliberately old-fashioned (novels, speeches, documents).\n- **Nごとき** (#98, no の): *a mere N* — contempt or humility: 小鬼ごとき (*a mere little demon*). Without の, ごとき reads as belittling, so a simile needs の: ✓鉄のごとき信念, ✗鉄ごとき信念.\n- **〜んばかり（に）** (#73): *as if about to*: 泣かんばかりに頼む (*beg, almost in tears*) — something nearly happens; かのごとく compares with something that is not the case.\n\nJLPT tip: match the image to a fitting result — 彗星のごとく現れ → わずか1年で大スターとなった (*became a big star in just a year*); 猫のごとく → 足音も立てずに消えていった (*vanished without a sound*); 何事もなかったかのごとく → 平然と食事を続けた (*calmly went on eating*); 安らかに眠るがごとく → 一生を終える (*end one's life peacefully, as if falling asleep*).",
          see: [73, 98],
