          deepDive: "**Nにして** is a formal, written equivalent of the particle **で** that marks the time, stage or circumstances in which something happens, putting **special emphasis** on that noun (the book: 状況や状態・様子について特にはっきり示したい). 一瞬にして消え去った = 一瞬で消え去った, but more vivid: *vanished in a single instant*.\n\nConnection: **N + にして**. The nouns are limited, and most uses are set phrases:\n- **Speed / time**: 一瞬にして (*in an instant*), 一夜にして (*overnight*: 一夜にして有名になった *became famous overnight*), ローマは一日にして成らず (*Rome wasn't built in a day*).\n- **Age / stage of life**: 四十にして惑わず (*at forty, I had no more doubts* — Confucius), 50歳にして初めて… (*not until fifty did …*) — it often stresses that something came surprisingly early or late.\n- **Point in a plan**: 志半ばにして (*with one's goals only half achieved*), almost always about dying or falling ill before finishing one's life's work.\n- **Fixed adverbs**: 幸いにして (*fortunately*), 不幸にして (*unfortunately*), 往々にして (*often*), 居ながらにして (*without leaving one's seat*; cf. **〜ながら（に）** #92).\n\nConstraints: ordinary nouns don't take it: ✗電車にして行く → ✓電車で行く (*go by train*); ✗図書館にして勉強した → ✓図書館で. It suits dramatic changes and turning points, not routine settings.\n\nRegister: formal and literary (formal icon). In conversation use で or plain adverbs (すぐに, 幸い).\n\nDon't confuse it with **AにしてB** (#23), *both A and B* — two sides of the same subject: 彼は大学教授にして人気作家でもある (*he is a university professor and a popular novelist as well*); 働き者にして美人 (*hardworking and beautiful*). #23 links two attributes (nouns or な-adjective stems); #102 marks the circumstance (*in / at*). A related set phrase is **Nにして初めて** (*only N can*): 名人にして初めてできる技 (*a technique only a master can pull off*).\n\nCommon mistake: taking 幸いにして or 一瞬にして as *making something into* (the にする sense: 部屋を倉庫にして *turn the room into a storeroom*). Here にして is just a weighty で.\n\nJLPT tip: a blank after 一瞬, 一夜, 幸い, 志半ば or an age (〜歳) in a formal sentence → にして. In the sample: 暗くなっていた空が、一瞬にして満月の明るさを取り戻した (*in an instant the darkened sky regained the brightness of the full moon*).",
          see: [23, 92],
